{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Decoded the rejected configuration entries and the operands answer the last open question about the mechanism. The rejection operand now includes each ConfChangeV2's transition and change list, and the draft-side run shows the log at the rejecting nodes to be '1:t0 2:t2(tr=1,1:4) 3:t0 4:t2(tr=1,0:4)': index 2 is a joint-enter change that removes node 4, index 4 is a joint-enter change that adds node 4, and neither is a leave-joint entry. So the committed history enters a joint configuration with a change that removes a non-member (a no-op for the voter set) and then enters another joint configuration from within it. The AutoLeave promise is what fails to break that cycle: appliedTo proposes the leave-joint change only when the applying node is the leader, so a joint change whose proposer is displaced before it applies its own change leaves the cluster joint with nobody having proposed the leave, and the next leader's guard then sees a non-joint tracker plus a stale pendingConfIndex and appends the second joint-enter change, which every node applying the log in order subsequently rejects. This replaces the earlier, coarser explanation with one fitted to the decoded entries and consistent with the deterministic guard observation in the previous receipt. Because these operands are draft-side, the submission requests isolated execution of the harness with the decoding instrumentation (transition and change list per EntryConfChangeV2 within the observed range, capped at eight entries, alongside the existing applied_before/applying_before/reapplied_entry and node state, with counters unchanged), and states in the assumptions what the operands still do not show - which node proposed each entry, or whether that proposer was displaced before applying it - so the reason no leave was appended remains an inference from the source condition plus the decoded entries rather than something this receipt observes. The asserted availability defect and its earlier receipts are unchanged by this addition.\"}"}}
